globs: tests/**,**/*Tests.fs,**/*Test*.fs,src/Shared/**,src/Client/**
---

Write tests for new functionality.

Transform tasks into verifiable goals:
- "Add validation" → write tests for invalid inputs, then make them pass
- "Fix the bug" → write a test that reproduces it, then make it pass
- "Refactor X" → ensure tests pass before and after

Use Test Driven Development: write test, stop for review; make test pass, stop for review; refactor, stop for review.

All logic should be in Shared/, if possible, based on library dependencies.
Any complex logic should be testable purely where possible. (If it depends on ambiguous browser behavior, it isn't.)
We are not testing to the browser.

Run targeted tests related to your edit as a foreground process.  Do not use vscode's test runner because it hangs.  Instead use dotnet test, specifying your test set.

If you edited Client dependencies (`src/Shared/`, `src/Client/`, or anything the Client fsproj references, including Shared documents when that project is in the Client graph), `dotnet test` on Shared.Tests is not enough. Run `./scripts/client.sh build` (Fable and esbuild). `/ambit` serves `Program.bundle.js`; `dotnet fable` alone is not sufficient. A Fable failure is a real failure, not a skip.

# Running all tests (including all tests of any one project) 
Subagents should never run all tests, because it takes a very long time.
The main agent may run all tests as a background process.  If the main agent does, it should filter all output except errors.

